{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the serpent.</b> This is Satan, which is the evil inclination (Bava Batra 16a), whose harm is great though little of him is seen. For a thing is indeed called by the name of something that resembles it — as a king is called a lion, as it says: עָלָה אַרְיֵה מִסֻּבְּכוֹ (Jeremiah 4:7), and harmful enemies are called serpents and vipers that have no charm, as it says: הִנְנִי מְשַׁלֵּחַ בָּכֶם נְחָשִׁים צִפְעוֹנִים וְכוּ׳ (Jeremiah 8:17). In this manner Scripture here calls the evil inclination that causes sin a serpent, because it resembles a serpent, whose benefit in existence is very slight while its harm is great though little of it is seen. And they said, of blessed memory (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 13), that Samael was riding upon it — that is, the desiring power that causes sin does this through the mediation of the imaginative power, which brings before it images of material pleasures that divert from the path of perfection directed by the blessed El. For indeed the desiring power together with the images of pleasures it brings are subservient to the physical powers that act and frustrate the intention and will of the blessed El when the intellectual power does not stand against them and efface them, as they said, of blessed memory (Jerusalem Talmud Berakhot 1:8): עֵינָא וְלִבָּא סַרְסוּרֵי דְחֶטְאָה (the eye and the heart are agents of sin). Concerning this He warned, saying: וְלֹא תָתוּרוּ אַחֲרֵי לְבַבְכֶם וְאַחֲרֵי עֵינֵיכֶם (Numbers 15:39)." "<b>Was more cunning than every beast of the field.</b> For the imaginative power that conveys images of pleasures to the desiring power was stronger in them than in other living beings, as they said (Sukkah 52b): כָּל הַגָּדוֹל מֵחֲבֵרוֹ יִצְרוֹ גָּדוֹל מִמֶּנּוּ (whoever is greater than his fellow, his inclination is greater than his fellow's)." "<b>And he said to the woman.</b> For her weak intellect neglected to reflect and did not stand against the false imagination, and her imaginative power then depicted that indeed <b>even though Elohim said</b> — that even though Elohim said <b>you shall not eat</b> from the tree of knowledge, <b>lest you die</b> — none of this would come true. Thus it says that when the serpent — that is, the imagination — began to raise such a doubt, the woman, with her weak intellect, said:"], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:28:53.680735332Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}